Problem 2b: Flat-field correction
I applied the normalized flat field to all 9 previously sky-subtracted
object images.

The corrected science data were calculated by dividing the
sky-subtracted object data by the normalized flat field.

There were 291 zero or otherwise invalid flat-field pixels. These
pixels cannot produce a valid flat correction, so I used a mask with
np.divide() and stored np.nan at those locations instead of allowing
divide-by-zero or invalid-value warnings.

