gaussian.py compatibility fix
-----------------------------
For one fit, scipy.optimize.leastsq() returned a valid solution but was
unable to calculate a covariance matrix. In this situation cov was None
and the original gaussian.py attempted to calculate:

np.diagonal(cov)

which caused an error.

I modified only the local copy of gaussian.py with a comment beginning:

# FIXED: 2026-10-26

When cov is None, the routine now returns NaN parameter uncertainties
while retaining the fitted Gaussian parameters.


Problem 3b: Photometry parameters
----------------------------------
I calculated the average of the nine fitted stellar widths:

Average fitted stellar width =
0.8856099184391977 pixels

I then selected the following photometry parameters as multiples of the
average fitted width:

Aperture radius:
3 sigma = 2.656829755317593 pixels

Inner sky-annulus radius:
5 sigma = 4.428049592195988 pixels

Outer sky-annulus radius:
8 sigma = 7.084879347513581 pixels

Photometry subimage size:
20 sigma, rounded upward to an odd integer = 19 pixels

The aperture radius was chosen to include nearly all of the Gaussian
stellar flux without unnecessarily increasing the background area.

The inner sky-annulus radius was placed sufficiently far from the
stellar profile to minimize contamination from the star.

The outer sky-annulus radius provides a larger number of pixels for
estimating the local sky background.

The photometry subimage size was chosen to contain the complete stellar
aperture and sky annulus with additional surrounding space.


